WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:08.134
At eight o'clock the next morning, we released the small boat from its hiding place atop the Nautilus . The sea was fairly

2
00:00:08.143 --> 00:00:14.539
calm. With Conseil and me rowing, Ned steered us safely past the coral reef that surrounded the island of

3
00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:17.880
Gilboa. I was overwhelmed at setting foot on land

4
00:00:17.881 --> 00:00:23.285
again. Ned strode across the sand as if he were an explorer taking possession of the

5
00:00:23.286 --> 00:00:30.690
island. It was hard to believe that only two months had passed since we had become "passengers onboard the Nautilus " in Captain

6
00:00:30.691 --> 00:00:34.952
Nemo's words. In reality we were prisoners of the

7
00:00:34.953 --> 00:00:38.356
submarine's commander. Within a few minutes we had plunged into a

8
00:00:38.357 --> 00:00:45.333
deep forest. While I was impressed by the beautiful mimosa and hibiscus flowers, Ned concentrated on getting something to

9
00:00:45.334 --> 00:00:49.849
eat. He discovered a coconut palm and retrieved some of the

10
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:55.690
fruit. After he broke open a coconut with his hatchet, we drank the milk and ate the meat with

11
00:00:55.691 --> 00:00:58.016
gusto. "Excellent!" proclaimed

12
00:00:58.017 --> 00:01:00.595
Ned. "Exquisite!" said

13
00:01:00.596 --> 00:01:08.333
Conseil. "Professor, I don't think that Captain Nemo would object to our bringing some coconuts onboard the Nautilus , do you?"

14
00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:13.984
said Ned. "I don't think he'd mind," I said, "but he won't taste them because they come from

15
00:01:13.985 --> 00:01:16.658
the land." "Too bad for him,"

16
00:01:16.659 --> 00:01:21.658
said Conseil. "And all the better for us because there will be more for us to eat,"

17
00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:27.150
said Ned. "I have only one note of caution," I said as Ned began to attack another

18
00:01:27.151 --> 00:01:35.975
coconut tree. "Coconuts are delicious, but before filling our boat with them, let's explore and see if this island produces any other

19
00:01:35.976 --> 00:01:40.134
useful things. Fresh vegetables would be a welcome addition to our diet onboard

20
00:01:40.135 --> 00:01:49.206
the Nautilus ." "You are right," said Conseil. "I propose that we save three spaces on our little boat: one for fruits, one for vegetables, and

21
00:01:49.207 --> 00:01:54.968
one for meat. And speaking of meat, I have yet to see even the smallest example of an animal

22
00:01:54.969 --> 00:01:58.143
on this island." "Conseil, you must be

23
00:01:58.144 --> 00:02:03.952
patient," said Ned. For two hours we walked through the forest until we came upon a grove

24
00:02:03.953 --> 00:02:08.881
of breadfruit trees. Ned was familiar with the breadfruit, having eaten it during his

25
00:02:08.882 --> 00:02:11.453
numerous voyages. He became very excited at the sight

26
00:02:11.454 --> 00:02:17.690
of it. "Professor, I will die if I don't taste some of this breadfruit right now,"

27
00:02:17.691 --> 00:02:24.095
said Ned. "Taste as much as you want," I said. "We're here to explore the island and the food

28
00:02:24.096 --> 00:02:26.841
it provides." "It won't take long,"

29
00:02:26.842 --> 00:02:31.087
Ned promised. While Conseil and I gathered breadfruit, Ned got a

30
00:02:31.088 --> 00:02:36.349
fire going. Conseil brought a dozen breadfruit to Ned, who cut them into

31
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:41.912
thick slices. "You will see how good this is, Professor," said Ned. "Have you ever eaten

32
00:02:41.913 --> 00:02:45.325
it before?" "No, Ned." I wasn't sure that I would

33
00:02:45.326 --> 00:02:53.333
like it. "Well then, prepare yourself for something delicious. If you don't come back for more, then I'm no longer the king of

34
00:02:53.334 --> 00:02:58.508
the harpooners." After a few minutes, the part of the fruit that was exposed to the fire was

35
00:02:58.509 --> 00:03:04.150
completely roasted. The interior looked like a white pastry, and I must confess that Ned

36
00:03:04.151 --> 00:03:09.404
was right. It was absolutely delicious, and I ate it with

37
00:03:09.405 --> 00:03:15.730
great enthusiasm. We continued to travel through the forest, gathering bananas, pineapples, beans,

38
00:03:15.731 --> 00:03:20.222
and yams. At five o'clock we loaded our small boat and rowed away

39
00:03:20.223 --> 00:03:24.484
from shore. As we pulled up next to the Nautilus , no one came

40
00:03:24.485 --> 00:03:32.230
to greet us. The black hull seemed deserted. We loaded our provisions onboard and returned to our cabins, where we found

41
00:03:32.231 --> 00:03:36.801
supper waiting. The next morning there was nothing new onboard, no sign

42
00:03:36.802 --> 00:03:40.722
of life. Our small boat rested where we had left it next to

43
00:03:40.723 --> 00:03:48.801
the submarine. Again we rowed out to the island. Ned hoped to have better luck with regard to hunting, so we decided to visit another part of

44
00:03:48.802 --> 00:03:55.031
the island. After crossing a large prairie, we came to a small forest filled with brightly

45
00:03:55.032 --> 00:04:00.198
colored birds. "These are only small birds," said Conseil in a

46
00:04:00.199 --> 00:04:04.619
disappointed tone. "Yes, but they can be eaten if you're hungry enough,"

47
00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:10.523
said Ned. We decided to keep walking and soon came across some larger, more

48
00:04:10.524 --> 00:04:16.365
beautiful birds. "Birds of paradise!" I exclaimed as the birds took off in

49
00:04:16.366 --> 00:04:23.261
undulating flight. We must have used half our ammunition trying to bring one down, but we were unsuccessful. Hunger drove

50
00:04:23.262 --> 00:04:30.253
us on. Finally Conseil shot two pigeons for our breakfast. While the birds roasted, Ned prepared

51
00:04:30.254 --> 00:04:37.404
some breadfruit. "These birds are excellent," he said, "but they are only side dishes. I won't be happy until I get

52
00:04:37.405 --> 00:04:43.832
some cutlets." I knew that I wouldn't be happy until I got a bird of paradise—not to eat but to keep for

53
00:04:43.833 --> 00:04:46.999
my collection. After eating we

54
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:55.078
continued walking. Some harmless snakes glided out of our path. Birds of paradise fled at our approach and I despaired at ever

55
00:04:55.079 --> 00:05:03.150
getting one. But Conseil, who was walking ahead of me, suddenly uttered a cry of triumph and held up a magnificent

56
00:05:03.151 --> 00:05:11.809
specimen. "Bravo, Conseil!" I cried. "If only I could bring this bird back to Paris and display it at the zoo in the botanical

57
00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.380
garden. How did you ever manage to catch

58
00:05:14.381 --> 00:05:18.555
it?" "If you examine the bird, you will see that I don't deserve any

59
00:05:18.556 --> 00:05:24.476
credit. This bird has been eating nutmeg, which has made it quite drunk and easy to

60
00:05:24.477 --> 00:05:29.634
catch." No sooner did I get my wish, than Ned got his: a large

61
00:05:29.635 --> 00:05:35.705
hog. We spent the rest of the day exploring the island, and Ned also managed to shoot some small

62
00:05:35.706 --> 00:05:39.221
kangaroos. By six o'clock we were back at the

63
00:05:39.222 --> 00:05:46.880
beach. Our small boat was still there and the Nautilus looked like a long rock emerging from the waves two miles

64
00:05:46.881 --> 00:05:50.444
away. Ned quickly got down to the business of preparing

65
00:05:50.445 --> 00:05:55.865
supper. The hog, grilling on the coals, soon scented the air with a delicious

66
00:05:55.866 --> 00:06:05.095
smell. We had an excellent supper. Besides the hog, we dined on pigeons, breadfruit, mangoes, pineapples, and coconut

67
00:06:05.096 --> 00:06:09.420
milk. "Suppose we don't return to the Nautilus tonight?" said

68
00:06:09.421 --> 00:06:12.579
Conseil. "Suppose we never return?" added

69
00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:17.564
Ned. Just then a stone fell at our feet and cut short the

70
00:06:17.565 --> 00:06:21.174
conversation. My hand stopped in the midst of putting food into my

71
00:06:21.175 --> 00:06:26.579
mouth. We scanned the edge of the forest without moving but didn't see anything.

72
00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:32.492
"Stones don't fall from the sky unless they're meteorites," observed Conseil.

73
00:06:32.493 --> 00:06:37.396
A second stone knocked the pigeon's leg from Conseil's hand, proving him right.

74
00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:41.896
All three of us stood up and shouldered our guns, ready to answer any attack.

75
00:06:41.897 --> 00:06:45.404
"Maybe it was just an ape?" suggested Ned.

76
00:06:45.405 --> 00:06:50.317
"No, look." Conseil pointed toward the woods. "Natives."

77
00:06:50.318 --> 00:06:56.721
About twenty natives, armed with slingshots and bows and arrows, appeared on the edge of the woods.

78
00:06:56.722 --> 00:06:59.737
They were only about a hundred feet from us.

79
00:06:59.738 --> 00:07:01.634
"To the boat!" I cried.

80
00:07:01.635 --> 00:07:10.794
Our boat was moored about sixty feet away. We started running as fast as we could while stones and arrows fell thickly around us.

